hat you  mentioned earlier are great I'm wearing  some kind of second-hand fashion so I  know that's one I've benefited from  myself but I do worry when we think  about flying taxis or more electric cars  and more batteries that we maybe miss a  conversation of how we can make things  like public transport more affordable  and accessible because that's just as  important a climate solution as well as  advancing in a technological way and so  I just really hope that some of the  money also goes towards advancing Tech  wise the systems that we already have  and not just thinking about new design  tools solutions that are kind of really  futuristic and fun Stuart Roberts do you  agree with that does there need to be  more of a joined up conversation and  more of a focus on reducing consumption  rather than relying on on new wysitech  ideas  yeah I think no it's a it's a  combination of both and for me I see  some really exciting Technology  Solutions in agriculture whether that be  feed additives that can reduce methane  whether that be methane tractors or  solar-powered robots to weed crops I  think that's all really exciting stuff  but for me it's also about uh not just  looking at Tech to solve this there's a  consumption side as Laura said but it's  also about looking at things like  particularly in agriculture carbon  sequestration so how can we take that  carbon out of the atmosphere through our  soil through our grassland so Tech it  can be exciting uh it could be fun uh  but but equally it's not the sole  solution and but isn't there an issue  Laura of keeping people on side some of  these exciting innovative ideas will  allow people to carry on their lives as  they were before but in a Greener way it  doesn't doesn't technology help them do  that and you've got to keep the public  committed  I would maybe worry that we're really  struggling to actually live the way that  we currently live at a sustainable rate  and I'm not really sure Green Tech will  be the solution for that actually I  would love to see this investment into  ways that we can do things in a more  sustainable way and kind of returning  back to some of the more simpler  Solutions but we can also have some  really fun Tech Solutions within the  systems that we already have instead of  just trying to create new Industries  above the ones that we already have and  I think it's just about shifting that  that mindset and although it might not  be as fun it's still possible and it  still allows for a lot of growth and a  lot of economic growth as well and I'm  just going to remind everyone of what  John Kerry said Stuart he rather  controversially said that 50 of the  reductions we have to make by 2050 or  2045 going to come from Technologies we  don't yet have I mean a lot of people  disputed that figure but do you think  that governments are caught up with this  idea of being over reliant on technology  as well  yeah I think for me it's that about that  balance so I think technology will help  us on that Journey absolutely and I can  see some areas uh in agriculture where  where there will be technology in future  to solve things we don't even know about  it yet but some of it is also about  looking at just how we do things so for  me the classic is the Hedgerow we can  sequester a lot more carbon in our  hedgerows by managing them slightly  different they add to our landscape but  they're maybe not quite as sexy and  exciting as some of the tech but  actually you can have a as big if not  bigger impact  um and and those are some of the areas  we've got to spend equal amounts of  resources and time on I've never thought  of hedgerows as being sexy but thank you  for for raising that possibility in my  mind and we're kind of kind of sticking  with that subject a little bit next  because we heard earlier in the program  about how the National Trust and other  big landowners have signed a pact to  restore wildlife habitats and cut carbon  emissions now the groups represent  around a third of English land so is  there something that almost can get  behind and how does the idea of  improving biodiversity fit in with  Industrial Level agriculture so Stuart  clearly a question of you first of all  and about 70 of land in England is  agricultural isn't it so is this  something you think that farmers can get  behind  well I think it's something that farmers  have already been behind actually when I  go back to our 2014 Net Zero aspiration  for agriculture uh three four years ago  now we started that I worry sometimes  that that we've become one-dimensional  so actually we look at biodiversity or  we look at water quality or we look at  uh carbon and then we'll sort of add  food into it as well for me what Farmers  need to do going forward is deliver all  of those things produce fantastic foods  but also deliver on biodiversity carbon  and everything else we expect  if you're trying to make a profit and  you've got to give over a load of your  land to biodiversity  as I said the key for me is about  actually lots of these areas there's a  sweet spot where by using our resources  more efficiently by thinking about how  we use our land and which bits of land  we use we can absolutely deliver on food  on the environment on biodiversity where  I worry is actually this sort of  wholesale we'll just turn it all over to  Nature we'll import our food from  somewhere else in the world and actually  just export our environmental footprint  British Farmers want to do both those  things and I think is is really exciting  for the future Laura how important are  these nature-based Solutions and do you  feel that farmers are doing enough  I think farmers are doing a lot and you  can see that through the various  different initiatives that they have you  know I'm from Scotland and it's really  fun going all over the country and  seeing the different solutions that  farmers have but I think one of the  important things that we need to do is  also support them support them in both  the education and in the finance for  doing some of these Solutions because  they are the custodians of lots of the  land that we have here in the UK and we  need to be supporting them because we  are also their customers and we really  want to buy food that has been  sustainably made and so we can all be  playing our part alongside them to help  deliver these nature-based solutions for  the land that we call home here in the  UK very briefly do you sometimes feel  like you're painted as the bad guys you  know agriculture responsible for lots of  methane emissions very briefly does that  feel like it to you  too often agriculture scene is the  problem whereas I see it as absolutely  part of the solution to biodiversity to  carbon but also to producing fantastic  food in a fantastic landscape okay  Stuart Roberts Laura young really good  to talk to you both thanks very much for  joining us this evening  and that's everything from us for today  on tomorrow's show I'll be joined by  Environmental Lawyer Tessa Khan and Mike  furnace Lee one of the country's leading  experts in carbon footprinting do stay  tuned though we've got Sky News Tonight  coming up for you next including covid  booster vaccines are going to be  extended to everyone aged over 18 has  two more cases of the Omicron variant of  covid-19 are detected in England but  that's it from me thanks for watching  and I'll see you next time  thank you  [Music]
 

